Therefore the definition of \(V_{h}\) implies that

(2.33) \[(P_{h}\omega-\omega_{h},\theta_{h})=0\ \ \ \ \ \forall v_{h}=(\operatorname{ \mathbf{curl}}\phi_{h},\theta_{h})\in V_{h}.\]

As a consequence,

(2.34) \[\|\omega_{h}-\theta_{h}\|_{0,\Omega}\leq\|P_{h}\omega-\theta_{h}\|_{0,\Omega} \ \ \ \ \ \forall v_{h}=(\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\phi_{h},\theta_{h})\in V_{h}\]

and the equivalence of the norms \(|.|\) and \(\|\cdot\|_{\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\chi}}}\) on \(V_{h}\) yields:

\[\|u_{h}-v_{h}\|_{\widetilde{\boldsymbol{\chi}}}\leq C\{\|P_{h}\omega-\omega\| _{0,\Omega}+|u-v_{h}|\}\ \ \ \ \ \ \forall v_{h}\in V_{h}.\]

This proves (2.30).

The bound (2.31) follows immediately from (2.30) and (1.26). 

_Remark 2.2_.: Formula (2.34) provides a slightly sharper estimate for \(|u-u_{h}|\):

\[|u-u_{h}|\leq 2\inf_{v_{h}\in V_{h}}|u-v_{h}|+\|\omega-P_{h}\omega\|_{0,\Omega}.\]

Now we turn to the pressure. To solve for the pressure, we introduce a fourth finite-dimensional space:

(2.35) \[Q_{h}\subset W^{1,\star}(\Omega)\cap L^{2}_{0}(\Omega)\]

with which we propose a straightforward discretization of Problem (2.21):

_Find \(p_{h}\in Q_{h}\) solution of_

(2.36) \[(\operatorname{\mathbf{grad}}p_{h},\operatorname{\mathbf{grad}}q_{h})=( \operatorname{\mathbf{f}}-v\operatorname{\mathbf{curl}}\omega_{h},\operatorname {\mathbf{grad}}q_{h})\ \ \ \ \ \forall q_{h}\in Q_{h}.\]

For a fixed \(\omega_{h}\), this square system of linear equations has a unique solution because the fact that \(\operatorname{\mathbf{grad}}p=0\) and \(p\in L^{2}_{0}(\Omega)\) implies that \(p=0\).

_Remark 2.3_.: Problems (2.29) and (2.36) can be combined in a single one:

_Find \(\psi_{h}\in\Phi_{h}\), \(\omega_{h}\in\Phi_{h}\) and \(p_{h}\in Q_{h}\) such that_

